5.text:
PRIVATE REFLECTION: The problem anchors the indexing with the explicit example ch.20 ↔ {{1,3,4},{2},{5}}. In restricted growth notation that is RGS 1 2 1 1 3. In 1-based lexicographic order of RGS length 5, 1 2 1 1 3 is the 18th string, so the convention used in the prompt is rank_lex = c − 2, i.e. c = rank+2, and ch.2 ↔ 11111. Hence p(c)=c−2.

Therefore c=39 ⇔ p=37. Ranks 1-15 have a2=1, 16-35 have a2=2 with a3=1,2. The a2=2,a3=3 block starts at rank 36:
36: 1 2 3 1 1
37: 1 2 3 1 2
38: 1 2 3 1 3
So rank 37 is RGS 1 2 3 1 2.

a1=1 → {1}
a2=2 → {2}
a3=3 → {3}
a4=1 → {1,4}
a5=2 → {2,5}
In increasing-minimum canonical order: {{1,4},{2,5},{3}}, the 1-4 | 2-5 | 3 kōzu.

Current round 2 is unanimous on this c-2 reading. The off-by-one literature convention p=c-1 is repeatedly noted: it maps ch.39 to the successive RGS 1 2 3 1 3 ↔ {{1,4},{2},{3,5}}, i.e. rank 38 under the c-2 convention, and would make ch.20 correspond to RGS 12112, contradicting the stated example. Earlier divergences about decreasing-block order, 2-block first patterns, {{1,3},{2},{4,5}}, {{1,2,3,5},{4}} etc., are inconsistent with the ch.20 anchor. Unresolved historical note remains that many published tables use the p=c-1 shift and quote {{1,4},{2},{3,5}} for Makibashira/Kobai. Under the problem’s explicit anchor the mathematically consistent answer is {{1,4},{2,5},{3}}.

